和11028所医院和诊所倒塌；550万农村房屋和86万城市房屋被毁或受损严重。四川、甘肃两省受灾最严重，直接经济损失估计约相当于四川省1122亿美元，甘肃省71亿美元。\n方式\n为了及时高效地制定震后重建规划，中国政府在地震发生后迅速开展了灾害损失、地质和环境评估工作。国务院颁布的《汶川地震灾后恢复重建总体规划》为全力推进灾后恢复重建奠定了基础。\n中国政府采取了各种创新机制保障恢复重建所需要的资金，充分利用包括公共与私营部门合作、税收优惠、动员公众支持等各种方式。中央政府建立了灾后重建对口支援机制，承担对口支援任务的省市与各自对口支援的受灾县市“结对”，负责为其提供资金和其他方式的援助。除硬件建设外，灾后重建规划也很重视能力建设，通过组织加强应急准备和培训项目，建立加强社区的自然灾害抵御能力。\n世行支持\n为了帮助地震灾区人民开展灾后重建和落实《汶川地震灾后恢复重建总体规划》，世界银行向中国政府提供紧急贷款7.1亿美元，用于甘肃（2亿美元）和四川（5.1亿美元）两省的灾后恢复重建。\n汶川地震恢复重建项目为四川、甘肃两省灾区的受损基础设施的重建和扩建提供资金，建设内容包括道路桥梁、供水及环境卫生、城市固体废弃物管理和收集处理、排水系统、防洪设施、边坡保护及稳定、运河航道恢复等，还包括乡镇卫生院、妇幼保健中心、县医院、社区防疫中心等卫生设施。此外，项目还为甘肃省灾区中小学和职业技术学校的恢复重建提供设施设备和能力建设。项目的目标是基础设施、医疗卫生和教育服务至少恢复到地震前的水平，并在适当情况下扩大服务范围，提高服务质量，同时降低面临地震、洪水等自然灾害的脆弱性。\n此外，世界银行的私营部门窗口国际金融公司为四川地震灾区的地方政府和金融机构提供咨询建议，帮助在灾后加强投资环境、吸引外来投资和支持恢复重建。国际金融公司共投资了六个项目，总金额达1.2亿美元，包括出资160万美元参股绵阳市商业银行设立的北川富民村镇银行，扩大农户和微小企业的融资渠道。\n全球减灾与恢复基金（GFDRR）赠款资助国际专家参与汶川地震项目准备，并为项目区的学校教职员工和医疗机构工作人员组织培训，加强学校和医院的灾害应急准备和应对能力全球减灾与恢复基金还协助中国政府对《汶川地震灾后恢复重建总体规划》以及农村重建、城乡住房、公共服务与基础设施、工业恢复、搬迁安置、组织与实施等六个具体方面进行审议评价，2012年6月发布了概要报告。\n全球环境基金（GEF）提供赠款100万美元调查和评估地震引起的危险化学品和废料泄漏可能造成的环境污染。\n\" 现在这个路修好了，我的生意好了很多。现在灰尘少了，噪音小了。希望以后再多修一些这些路，把我们三台县整得更加美好。 \"\n三台县的一位出租车司机\n成果\n汶川地震恢复重建项目自2009年中启动以来取得了显著进展。世行资金也带动了其他渠道的投资，帮助改变了灾区城市面貌、改善了服务和人民生活，提高了整体环境质量。具体项目成果如下：\n在四川省恢复重建道路110公里、雨水排水管网100公里、污水管网93公里、桥梁2公里、堤坝105公里、泵站2座、垃圾中转站1座、垃圾存放站3座和自来水厂2座。\n在四川省19个县市重建和扩建乡镇卫生院、妇幼保健院、县医院和社区防疫中心。截止到2012年11月，60个医疗卫生项目全部完成和投入运营，受益人口达982万人。\n在甘肃省恢复重建房屋、道路、供排水管网和环境卫生基础设施项目31个、学校6所、医院4所、桥梁1座、自来水厂1座和污水处理厂2座。\n恢复重建后的各项设施符合环境标准，力求污染最小化。在世行协助下，政府修复了遭受地震破坏的自然环境，在保护当地生态系统的同时也有助于减少未来的灾害。\n对地震灾区的历史文化遗产和城市独特风貌采取了具体的保护措施。\n面向未来\n世界银行将继续加强与中国政府在灾害风险管理方面的合作伙伴关系，向其他国家传播中国在灾害准备及救援方面的经验。在通过世行提供的全球减灾与恢复基金支持下，中国紧急救援培训中心在北京为印尼一些救援队举办了为期一周的应急救援培训，还出版了重建经验报告在国际上推广。\n受益人感言\n三台县的一位出租车司机：“现在这个路修好了，我的生意好了很多。现在灰尘少了，噪音小了。希望以后再多修一些这些路，把我们三台县整得更加美好。”\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：World Bank Sichuan earthquake reconstruction evaluation impact\n\n网页标题：Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\n\n网页链接：http://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/630011468216570128/pdf/ISR-Disclosable-P114107-12-12-2014-1418374161336.pdf\n\n网页内容：Project Development Objective (from Project Appraisal Document) To restore essential infrastructure, health, and education services to at least levels existing prior to the Wenchuan Earthquake, and where appropriate, to provide for expansion of services, while reducing the vulnerability to seismic and flood hazards and building capacity of local governments to manage the recovery program.\nHas the Project Development Objective been changed since Board Approval of the Project Objective? No\nPHRPDODEL\nName Previous Rating Current Rating\nComponents\nName\n1.1 Infrastructure Sichuan:(Cost $432.40 M)\n1.2 Health Sichuan:(Cost $57.60 M)\n1.3 Project Support Sichuan:(Cost $20.00 M)\n2.1 Infrastructure Gansu:(Cost $130.30 M)\n2.2 Health Gansu:(Cost $32.30 M)\n2.3 Education Gansu:(Cost $33.40 M)\n2.4 Project Support Gansu:(Cost $4.00 M)\nOverall Ratings\nPublic Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized\nThe World Bank Implementation Status \u0026 Results Report Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\n12/12/2014 Page 2 of 10\nPublic Disclosure Copy\nPublic Disclosure Copy\nProgress towards achievement of PDO  Moderately Unsatisfactory  Moderately Satisfactory\nOverall Implementation Progress (IP)  Moderately Unsatisfactory  Moderately Unsatisfactory\nOverall Risk Rating  - Substantial\nImplementation Status and Key Decisions\nIn Sichuan, restoration of essential infrastructure is largely satisfactory with 100% of water supply services completed and 90% of roads, with only one bridge remaining to be completed from the originally planned works. The health facilities have been 100% restored. The Loan closing date has been extended to allow for US$ 40 million in loan savings to be used for additional civil works in project cities to further augment achievement by expanding services. The detailed designs of the new subprojects are being finalized and bidding will start before the end of 2014. These new infrastructure projects are expected to be completed by February 2016.\nIn Gansu, restoration of essential water infrastructure has reached 75% of the total. A few roads in Cheng and Xihe County and the Middle School in Cheng County have to be completed and accepted. The Moba Gorge Dam and ancillary water supply facilities are the main remaining works. Full restoration of roads has reached 75% pending completion of these works. Delays stem from (a) slow progress on the Moba Gorge Dam due to delays in the construction of the river diversion tunnel, necessary to start full construction of the dam, (b) slow resettlement progress in Cheng and Xihe Counties; and (c) coordination issues. All schools have been substantially completed, but issues of finishing and cleanliness remain. The construction of health facilities is substantially completed, with only one hospital undergoing final acceptance. The Loan closing date was extended to May 2016 to allow for full completion of all existing and new civil works and to rectify outstanding quality problems. The project management offices (PMOs), PIUs and contract supervisors have to ensure the quality of works and conformity to original designs and technical specifications. Although most of the resettlement has been completed, the pending resettlement issues need to be addressed and Longnan PMO has to prepare regular progress reports to flag the problems.\nRisks\nSystematic Operations Risk-rating Tool\nRisk Category Rating at Approval Previous Rating Current Rating\nPolitical and Governance  - - Moderate\n Capacity Building Sichuan - Percentage of PMO/PIU staff trained in project management processes for earthquake reconstruction. (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 100.00 100.00 100.00\nDate 05-Dec-2008 30-May-2014 10-Dec-2014 30-Jun-2014\nThe World Bank Implementation Status \u0026 Results Report Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\n12/12/2014 Page 7 of 10\nPublic Disclosure Copy\nPublic Disclosure Copy\nPHINDIRITBL\n Infrastructure Gansu - Percentage of total project infrastructure assets (measured by sector with appropriate indicators) built to applicable government standards that address flood and seismic hazard (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 50.00 80.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 30-May-2014 10-Dec-2014 31-May-2016\nComments Few roads in Xihe County, Wudu District and Cheng County are pending completion due to unresolved resettlemetn issues. Everything will be completed by the new closing date.\nPHINDIRITBL\n Health Gansu - Percentage of total number of project health facilities built to applicable government standards that address flood and seismic hazards. (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 100.00 100.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 30-May-2014 10-Dec-2014 30-Jun-2014\nPHINDIRITBL\n Health Gansu - Percentage of restored project facilities that are fully equipped according to government standards. (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 70.00 90.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 30-May-2014 10-Dec-2014 31-May-2016\nComments Only the Cheng County Traditional Hospital is pending final acceptance, should be fully operational after Chinese New Year (March 1, 2015).\nEducation Gansu - Percentage of total number of project schools built to applicable government standards that address flood and seismic hazards. (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 100.00 100.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 30-May-2014 30-May-2014 30-Jun-2014\n Education Gansu - Percentage of...\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：World Bank Sichuan earthquake reconstruction evaluation impact\n\n网页标题：China: Building Back Stronger\n\n网页链接：https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2018/05/11/building-back-better-plus\n\n发布时间：2018-05-11T00:00:00+08:00\n\n网页内容：The project helped build the capacity of local governments, and laid the foundation for more sustainable development and improving the quality of life and resilience of communities.\nPost-Wenchuan Earthquake Reconstruction and Development, Ten Years On\nChallenge\nOn May 12, 2008, a 8.01 magnitude earthquake struck southwestern China, centered in Wenchuan...\nThe Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery Project restored and improved critical infrastructure, health, and education services in severely affected counties in China’s Sichuan and Gansu provinces, directly benefiting about 12.7 million people.\nResults\nThe project was implemented in 27 severely affected counties in Sichuan and Gansu between 2009 and 2014, and restored and enhanced access to essential infrastructure and other public services, such as water, sanitation, roads, health and education, but also greatly enhanced the services from pre-earthquake levels and contributed to rapid recovery and growth of the local economies, specifically:\nRoads. 300 roads were built or renovated to new seismic standards, with new traffic management system, drainage, lighting and landscaping.\n![](https://www.worldbank.org/content/dam/photos/780x439/2018/may-1/cn-wenchuan4.jpg)\nWater. Ten water supply plants were built with 230 km of water supply pipelines and four pumping stations, improving access to households and commercial and industrial users, as well as for urban fire-fighting purposes. This led to an increase of 25% in coverage of households and commercial buildings with a continuous 24-hour water supply compared to the pre-earthquake situation.\nWastewater. Nine wastewater treatment plants were rehabilitated or constructed, and 102.5 km of sewerage pipelines and 492.5 km of storm water drainage pipelines were built. As a result, the sewage treatment ratio in the project counties increased by over 50% compared to the pre-earthquake situation.\nSolid waste collection and disposal (Sichuan only). Investments under this project led to an average increase of 42% in the collection and disposal ratio of solid waste garbage compared to the pre-earthquake situation.\nHealth. 67 health facilities were built, equipped and are fully operational. The number of hospital beds in the project counties increased by 67% and 35% in Sichuan and Gansu respectively. The new facilities has greatly enhanced diagnostic and treatment capacities and provide a wider range of in-patient and out-patient medical services, resulting in higher admissions than before the earthquake.\n![](https://www.worldbank.org/content/dam/photos/780x439/2018/may-1/cn-wenchuan3.jpg)\nEducation (Gansu Only). Seven new schools were built and equipped with text books, computer equipment and laboratories, classroom, canteen and dormitory furniture, and sporting equipment. Training courses were provided for 450 principals, teachers and administrators, and covered a broad range of education topics, including new emergency management procedures.\nCapacity building. The provincial, municipal and county level governments of both provinces benefited from a broad range of training and capacity building activities related to the World Bank’s safeguards, financial management and procurement, as well as technical areas such as investigation, design and contract supervision, with a total of 3,701 training days completed.\nEconomic growth. Compared with pre-earthquake level, GDP grew between 117% and 198%, primarily driven by growth in the non-agricultural sectors. Secondary industries (including both industrial and construction sectors) increased between 116% and 286% and service sectors grew by 135% and 201%. Per capita income across the board increased between 90% and 154% in project counties.\nBank Group Contribution\nA US$710 million Emergency Loan was provided by the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD) to support recovery investments in Sichuan (US$510 million) and Gansu (US$200 million), focusing on the reconstruction of infrastructure such as roads, bridges, water supply, wastewater, solid waste, and health facilities in selected counties in both provinces, and also on the reconstruction of education facilities in Gansu.\nThe Bank mobilized US$1.5 million grant from the Global Facility for Disaster Reduction and Recovery (GFDRR) to support the preparation of policy notes, the involvement of international experts, and the training of teachers and school staff, as well as hospital staff. The GFDRR grant also supported the government to review and evaluate the implementation of the national reconstruction materplan and six sector-specific reconstruction plans.\nThe Bank also mobilized a Global Environment Facility (GEF) grant of US$1 million to support assessment of potential chemical contamination from industries affected by the earthquake.\nThe International Finance Corporation (IFC), the private sector arm of the World Bank Group, created credit lines for small and medium-sized businesses in the aftermath of the earthquake.\nPartners\nIn addition to GFDRR and GEF, the French Development Agency (AfD) provided a grant to support early engagement of French consultants who worked with the World Bank team to develop selection criteria and appraise subprojects, and help build early capacity of provincial project management offices. Finally, a GEF grant supported the assessment of the chemical contamination in the immediate aftermath of the earthquake.\nMoving Forward\nThe project supported the training of post-disaster recovery teams in the Asia-Pacific region, strengthened the part­nership with the government of China on disaster risk management activities, and disseminate China’s experience and expertise in disaster preparedness and relief to other countries in the region and worldwide. A report on the lessons learned from the reconstruction has been published for international dissemination and many of the lessons from the Wenchuan Earthquake reconstruction are being applied elsewhere.\nThe Lushan Earthquake struck Sichuan in 2013, almost five years after the Wenchuan Earthquake, and affected 2.2 million people. The World Bank-supported Lushan Earthquake Reconstruction and Risk Reduction Project has learned and incorporated lessons from the Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery Project.\nTo this day, the Wenchuan Earthquake serves an important reminder to other countries prone to earthquakes of the importance of measures ensuring construction quality and structural resilience of buildings and infrastructure. Moreover, the recovery process, its efficacy, and speed in Sichuan, serves an example of the large-scale reconstruction that aims not only to replace the lost and damaged assets but also to bring the reconstructed and new infrastructure to a higher quality standards, based on the upgraded building codes and regulations, and use the reconstruction to improve local economies and living standards of the affected population.\nBeneficiaries\nPan Li, Deputy County Mayor of Yanting: “Through implementation of the World Bank-supported project, our city’s environment has greatly improved, we have an expanding urban space and flourishing businesses and tourism. Compared to 2008, our GDP has increased by 127%, and urban per capita income by 180%. We have gained significant ecological, social and economic benefits. This is welcomed and appreciated by the people.”\nZhang Yufang, Resident of Cangxi: “The change is enormous. In the past, we had only dirt roads and lived in mud houses. Today where we live, what we eat, wear and use is all different.”\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：World Bank Sichuan earthquake reconstruction evaluation impact\n\n网页标题：Lushan Earthquake Reconstruction and Risk Reduction Project (P153548)\n\n网页链接：http://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099105012222216769/pdf/P1535480dc367708b0a4290c86ac98f9e3d.pdf\n\n网页内容：The World Bank Implementation Status \u0026 Results Report Lushan Earthquake Reconstruction and Risk Reduction Project (P153548)\n12/22/2022 Page 1 of 10\nLushan Earthquake Reconstruction and Risk Reduction Project (P153548)\nEAST ASIA AND PACIFIC | China | Urban, Resilience and Land Global Practice | IBRD/IDA | Investment Project Financing | FY 2017 | Seq No: 12 | ARCHIVED on 22-Dec-2022 | ISR53973 |\nImplementing Agencies: People's Republic of China, Sichuan Provincial Transportation Department Highway Bureau (TPPMO), Sichuan Urban Environment Project Office (UPPMO)\nKey Dates\nKey Project Dates\nBank Approval Date: 13-Oct-2016 Effectiveness Date: 06-Mar-2017\nPlanned Mid Term Review Date: 02-Dec-2019 Actual Mid-Term Review Date: 02-Dec-2019\nOriginal Closing Date: 30-Jun-2022 Revised Closing Date: 31-Dec-2023\npdoTable\nProject Development Objectives\nProject Development Objective (from Project Appraisal Document)\nThe project development objectives are to improve access to disaster resilient infrastructure and strengthen risk reduction in select municipalities of Sichuan Province and to improve emergency preparedness in Ya'an Municipality in Sichuan Province.\nHas the Project Development Objective been changed since Board Approval of the Project Objective?\nNo\nComponents Table\nName\nUpgrading and Risk Reduction of Rural Roads:(Cost $34.83 M) Upgrading of Priority Urban and Emergency Infrastructure:(Cost $375.71 M) Technical Assistance for Strengthening of Disaster Management and Preparedness in Ya'an Municipality:(Cost $22.11 M) Project Management and Capacity Building:(Cost $27.02 M)\nOverall Ratings\nName Previous Rating Current Rating\nProgress towards achievement of PDO Satisfactory Satisfactory\nOverall Implementation Progress (IP) Satisfactory Satisfactory\nOverall Risk Rating Moderate Moderate\nImplementation Status and Key Decisions\nThe Project Development Objective (PDO) is to improve access to disaster resilient infrastructure and to strengthen risk reduction in selected municipalities of Sichuan Province, and to improve emergency preparedness in Ya'an Municipality of Sichuan Province. This objective remains highly relevant to the national, local, and World Bank strategies for China. As of December 2022, 363,406 people in Ya’an Municipality benefit from improved access to urban infrastructure that is regarded as lifelines in the event of an emergency; 131,000 villagers are benefiting from two completed rural roads in Tianquan and Qionglai; 100,000 people in targeted urban areas are provided with improved access to roads; 86,000 people in targeted urban areas are provided with improved drainage conditions; and 61,400 people have improved access to emergency shelters. The project also has improved the urban resilience management of Ya’an Municipality by enhancing the capacity of local authorities in Shimian\nPublic Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized\nThe World Bank Implementation Status \u0026 Results Report Lushan Earthquake Reconstruction and Risk Reduction Project (P153548)\n12/22/2022 Page 2 of 10\nCounty to prepare for multi-hazards by setting up a disaster risk management system for the county. The project’s closing date has been extended to December 31, 2023 to allow completion of all remaining activities with upscaled outcomes.\nRisks\nSystematic Operations Risk-rating Tool\nRisk Category Rating at Approval Previous Rating Current Rating\nPolitical and Governance Low Low Low\nMacroeconomic Moderate Low Low\nSector Strategies and Policies Moderate Low Low\nTechnical Design of Project or Program Moderate Low Moderate\nInstitutional Capacity for Implementation and Sustainability Substantial Low Moderate\nFiduciary Moderate Low Low\nEnvironment and Social Moderate Moderate Moderate\nStakeholders Low Low Low\nOther -- Moderate Moderate\nOverall Substantial Moderate Moderate\nResults\nPDO Indicators by Objectives / Outcomes\nimprove access to disaster resilient infra., strengthen risk reduction and improve eme